\ SCANW.SEQ     Scan a list of words for a word         by Tom Zimmer
\ F-PC native port

comment:

        Works like the normal SCAN, but scans a list of WORDS for a WORD.
        That is you must divide the space to be searched by 2 to obtain
        the length to search

        F-PC native port: a "word" is now a CELL (4 bytes), because what
        SCANW searches (token lists for REF, cell arrays in BLOCK.SEQ)
        became cells. Divide the byte length by CELL (or use the cell
        count). The three CODE words are rewritten in high level Forth;
        results are the same as the 8086 string instructions gave.

comment;
                                        \ a1 = scan start address
                                        \ w1 = length to scan (cells)
                                        \ w2 = cell we are looking for
                                        \ a2 = address where w2 was found
                                        \ w3 = remaining search length (cells)
: scanw         ( a1 w1 w2 --- a2 w3 )  \ Scan array a1 for cell w2.
                >r
                begin   dup
                        if      over @ r@ <>
                        else    false
                        then
                while   1- swap cell+ swap
                repeat  r>drop ;


\ Scan for char BACKWARDS starting at addr+len, back through len bytes
\ before addr, returning addr' and len' of char.
\ (As the original code did: the bytes examined are addr, addr-1 ...
\ addr-len+1. Not found returns addr-len 0.)

: -scan         ( addr len char -- addr' len' )
                >r
                begin   dup
                        if      over c@ r@ <>
                        else    false
                        then
                while   1- swap 1- swap
                repeat  r>drop ;

\ Skip occurances of char BACKWARDS starting at addr, back through
\ addr-len, returning addr' and len' of char.

: -skip         ( addr len char -- addr' len' )
                >r
                begin   dup
                        if      over c@ r@ =
                        else    false
                        then
                while   1- swap 1- swap
                repeat  r>drop ;

